Z||000000||snafus87||12-25-2001||10:04 PM||||ok...I'm driving my car down the road the other day and everything is great...<P>Today I get in to go to the store and get some small things and decide to let the car warm up first...<P>When I come outside to leave (about 10 min after starting car) and prepare to leave, I can't get the damn thing into 1st gear...I thought I was going to break my shift arm trying...I finally shut the car off, put it in 1st and then started it (and it lurched forward a little bit).<P>Along the way, I'm fighting it VERY hard to get it to shift in and out of gear.  I finally resorted to "no-clutching" it and matching revvs...which was fine until I had to stop for a light...<P>I have NO idea what I'm doing when it comes to clutch stuff, so any advice would be welcomed...<P>If you need more info, just ask and I'll do my best to describe it...<P>I live in Lehighton, PA if anyone is interested in helping me fix this problem...<P>Thanks.<P>------------------<BR>JT Krupa<BR>85 GT<BR>(fastback conversion)<BR>180+ K miles<BR>Lehighton, PA||||reg||4
Z||000001||bocefas||12-25-2001||10:16 PM||||Check the level of fluid in your slave cyl. resivoir...I bet it's empty...If you're lucky, you can put some fluid and get your clutch to work temporarily, but chances are the seal blew out and you'll have to get a new one right away.  It's a pretty easy fix.||||reg||1
Z||000002||Ferrari_cdn||12-25-2001||10:16 PM||||Is it cold there?  I think you might have the same problem as I do, but I doubt it.  My clutch is great, except when it gets BITCHY cold here.  When it does, her shifting gets hard.  Can't get in first gear.  The clutch feels soft, I can press the clutch all the way in and it feels soft.  But when I try to pump the clutch, I get all the pressure back, and she will disengage easily.  But after leaving it alone for a few seconds, she will go soft again.  I think I am low in clutch fluid, or my clutch fluid turned into gel when it's -20C outside.  Try putting your car in neutral, and start the car.  Pump the clutch like 3-4 times fast then push the clutch in and shift to first, see if it is easy.  I will have to check my clutch fluid level in the morning when it's brighter outside.<P>------------------<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/ICEFierosig.gif"> AIM: CanadianFiero||||reg||1
Z||000003||snafus87||12-25-2001||10:22 PM||||checked it already (up front on driver's side, right??) and its full.||||reg||1
Z||000004||G-Nasty||12-26-2001||02:04 AM||||It sounds like you need a new slave cylinder.<BR>The book says to replace both the master and slave at once...but us poor fiero folk usually replace slave first...if that doesnt fix it simply replace master. Make sure you bleed correctly. If both parts fail to get 1st gear then you may have clutch/clutch fork/kinked or bad lines or cables. ||||reg||1
Z||000005||snafus87||12-26-2001||06:53 PM||||ok, I'll try the slave cylinder, but I did do the pumping the pedal 3 or 4 times real fast and that made it shift easier...<P>Could I just have gotten air in the system, or is that a sign of a slave blown out??<P>Either way, I have to bleed, and now my question becomes, HOW do I bleed the clutch system properly?  Is there a "best" way to do it?||||reg||5
Z||000006||johnt671||12-26-2001||07:04 PM||||I had a new clutch put in a few years ago, picked up the car and couldn't shift it unless I pumped it a few times.  Turned out the new slave cylinder was bad.  Put the old one on and was good to go.||||reg||1
Z||000007||snafus87||12-26-2001||07:12 PM||||would a slave used from any car work (4 or 5spd) or would I just be better buying a new one??||||reg||1
